sections.s2_technical:
To understand fencing, you need three identifiers. First, the transactional id, which you set with `transactional.id`, is a stable logical name you choose for a producer role, for example `settlement-tx-producer-1`. Second, the producer id, which the coordinator assigns and which maps to that transactional id. Third, the epoch, a number the coordinator attaches to the producer id and increments every time a producer initializes transactions under that transactional id. The combination of producer id and epoch is what the broker checks on every write.

The sequence of events for a zombie is as follows. A producer calls `producer.initTransactions()`, which registers its transactional id with the coordinator and gets producer id and epoch, say epoch five. It begins writing inside a transaction. Then a network partition isolates it. The coordinator or an orchestrator decides this instance is gone, and a replacement producer starts up with the same transactional id and calls `initTransactions()`. The coordinator sees an existing registration, bumps the epoch to six, and aborts any in-flight transaction from epoch five. Now the healthy producer owns epoch six.

When the partition heals, the zombie, still believing it holds epoch five, tries to send records or to commit. The broker compares the incoming epoch five against the current epoch six and rejects the request. The client receives a `ProducerFencedException` (or related fatal error). This exception is fatal and non-retriable by design: the correct response is to close that producer and not attempt to continue. In code you handle it like this:

```java
try {
    producer.beginTransaction();
    producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("settlements", key, value));
    producer.commitTransaction();
} catch (ProducerFencedException | OutOfOrderSequenceException | AuthorizationException fatal) {
    // Zombie detected or unrecoverable: must close, cannot continue.
    producer.close();
} catch (KafkaException e) {
    producer.abortTransaction(); // transient: safe to retry the transaction
}
```

A key correctness point is that fencing protects the atomicity of a transaction's commit. Any records the zombie wrote before being fenced that were part of an uncommitted transaction are never made visible, because consumers with `isolation.level=read_committed` only see records up to the last stable offset, and aborted or never-committed transactions are skipped. The commit marker from the zombie is what gets rejected, so its data is effectively erased from the consumer's point of view.

Several senior-level nuances matter. First, `enable.idempotence=true` is implied by transactions and provides a related but narrower guarantee: within a single producer session, the broker deduplicates retries using the producer id, epoch, and a per-partition sequence number, so a retry that the broker already persisted is acknowledged without being written twice. That stops duplicate writes caused by producer retries, but it is the epoch bump that stops a whole second zombie instance. Second, this only protects what happens inside Kafka: if your producer also writes to a database, Kafka's fencing does not roll back the database, which is why the transactional outbox pattern exists for cross-system exactly-once. Third, you must give each logical producer role a stable, unique transactional id; if two genuinely different producers accidentally share one transactional id, they will constantly fence each other and neither will make progress. Fourth, there is a hardening setting `transaction.timeout.ms`: if a producer holds an open transaction longer than this, the coordinator aborts it, which bounds how long a stuck or slow producer can block the last stable offset for read-committed consumers.

